in @prism

# @epistemologic/property/laws/causality
#
# A type is causal iff its values carry a partial order under which
# effects follow causes. For any two events e₁ → e₂ in a causal
# chain, e₁.tick ≤ e₂.tick within the same frame. Lamport's logical-
# clock condition (Lamport 1978) at the type layer.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-time-as-substrate-and-postgres-heritage.md
# §6 (the property table): causality is the partial-order extension
# of monotonicity. Where monotonicity verifies a single chain is
# non-decreasing, causality verifies that across all chains the
# effect-follows-cause relation holds. The load-bearing instance is
# @beam.beam: every beam in a path has emitted ≤ self.emitted; the
# whole pipeline forms a causal DAG.
#
# Per Lamport (1978) "Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a
# Distributed System": the happens-before relation is partial,
# transitive, and irreflexive. This property encodes the
# happens-before condition as a structural check on the type's
# event graph.
#
# Sibling properties: monotonicity(T) (the total-order within-frame
# baseline this builds on), frame_relativity(T) (cross-frame
# causality requires conversion through shard ancestry, just like
# cross-frame comparison does).

grammar @epistemologic/property/laws/causality {

  # The type's values carry a happens-before relation: partial,
  # transitive, irreflexive. Baseline structural requirement; without
  # happens-before, causality is undefined.
  happens_before(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # For every cause-effect pair (e₁, e₂) reachable in the type's
  # event graph, e₁.tick ≤ e₂.tick. The verifier walks the graph;
  # any back-edge in tick order fails the property. Lamport's
  # clock-condition.
  effects_follow_causes(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The causal order is preserved under the substrate's content
  # addressing: if e₁ → e₂, then e₁'s oid appears in e₂'s ancestor
  # chain. This is the structural condition that makes causality
  # verifiable from the storage layer alone, without re-running the
  # pipeline. Per @epistemologic/property/content_addressed: the
  # ancestor chain IS the causal DAG.
  content_preserves_order(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The combined property: the type is causal.
  # Pass iff happens_before, effects_follow_causes, and
  # content_preserves_order.
  causality(type) -> verdict { \ }
}

out happens_before
out effects_follow_causes
out content_preserves_order
out causality
out @epistemologic/property/laws/causality
